WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:03.690
Mira manages a fictional
apartment building.

2
00:00:03.690 --> 00:00:07.350
A winter energy bill arrives,
much higher than usual.

3
00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:10.444
Her first thought is to
replace the old heating unit.

4
00:00:10.584 --> 00:00:13.114
A bill can show cost and consumption.

5
00:00:13.114 --> 00:00:15.686
It cannot explain the cause by itself.

6
00:00:15.826 --> 00:00:19.530
A higher bill might reflect a
tariff change, not more energy.

7
00:00:19.530 --> 00:00:23.400
If use did rise, the cause could be
the equipment, settings,

8
00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:27.115
schedules, air leaks, how the building
is used, or the weather.

9
00:00:27.255 --> 00:00:29.060
That’s what an energy audit is for.

10
00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:32.260
It looks at the building’s
equipment and how it’s operated,

11
00:00:32.260 --> 00:00:36.165
ideally with a qualified assessor,
before ranking what to do.

12
00:00:36.305 --> 00:00:40.150
The U.S. Department of Energy
offers tools for this work.

13
00:00:40.150 --> 00:00:43.395
Its Audit Template is a web tool
for collecting building

14
00:00:43.395 --> 00:00:48.158
and audit information, following
an industry standard for energy audits.

15
00:00:48.298 --> 00:00:52.578
Its BuildingSync format gives
audit data a consistent structure,

16
00:00:52.578 --> 00:00:56.382
because data without a standard
is harder to use and compare.

17
00:00:56.522 --> 00:01:00.260
In Mira’s building, the energy
ribbons are traced room by room.

18
00:01:00.260 --> 00:01:03.804
One ribbon keeps running at
night, when nobody is there.

19
00:01:03.944 --> 00:01:07.660
Consistent data comes first:
the bills, the equipment list,

20
00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:09.310
and an operations diary.

21
00:01:09.310 --> 00:01:10.764
Then inspection.

22
00:01:10.764 --> 00:01:13.517
Then a ranked list of possible measures.

23
00:01:13.657 --> 00:01:16.277
An audit identifies opportunities.

24
00:01:16.277 --> 00:01:19.797
It doesn’t make the changes, and
it doesn’t guarantee savings.

25
00:01:19.797 --> 00:01:24.090
Weather, occupancy and operation
can all change the results.

26
00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:24.920
Doing the work,

27
00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:26.940
and measuring what happened afterwards,

28
00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:28.394
are separate steps.

29
00:01:28.534 --> 00:01:31.343
These are U.S. educational resources,

30
00:01:31.343 --> 00:01:32.886
not the rules where you live.

31
00:01:32.886 --> 00:01:35.144
Check what applies to your building.

32
00:01:35.284 --> 00:01:40.230
Before buying a new machine, follow
four steps: data, diagnosis, decision,

33
00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:41.818
and then verification.

34
00:01:41.958 --> 00:01:43.428
Ahmadreza Samadi.

35
